-- title:
--   Complex differentiability implies continuity along a vertical line
-- statement:
--   Let $f : \mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C}$ and let $s \in \mathbb{C}$ be a point at which $f$ is complex differentiable. Consider the restriction of $f$ to the vertical line through $s$, parametrized by the real variable $x$:
--
--   $$x \longmapsto f(\operatorname{Re}(s) + x\, i).$$
--
--   Then this real-parametrized function is continuous at $x = \operatorname{Im}(s)$ (the parameter value corresponding to the point $s$ itself).
--
--   This is a simple but frequently used bridge between complex-analytic hypotheses and the real-variable integration theory on vertical lines: contour integrals in the PNT+ development (e.g. the vertical integrals appearing in Perron/Mellin inversion) are Bochner integrals over $t \in \mathbb{R}$ of $f(\sigma + ti)$, and continuity of the integrand at individual points is deduced from complex differentiability of $f$ via this lemma.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/AlexKontorovich/PrimeNumberTheoremAnd/blob/f55e85551ac10e96d98262a354cfcaac2825f2da/PrimeNumberTheoremAnd/MediumPNT.lean#L730-L734

